{{wikipedia|dab=rouse}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[rouze]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|rowsen|lang=enm}}, {{term|rouzen|lang=enm}}, {{term|rusen||to rush out|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|non|en}} *{{term||rūsa|to storm out, rush|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*rūsaną||to bluster, be fierce, storm}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*(o)rewǝ-||to move, drive, agitate}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|rusa||to rush, hurry, dash, scurry|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|ruse||to rush|lang=da}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|rusen|rūsen|to race, rage|lang=dum}}, {{etyl|gml|-}} {{term|rūsen||to rush, bluster, make a clamour|lang=gml}}. More at {{l|en|rush}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɹaʊz/}}
* {{rhymes|aʊz}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# an [[arousal]]
# an official [[ceremony]] over drinks
#: ''And the King's '''rouse''' the heaven shall bruit again,''
#: ''Re-speaking earthly thunder.'' - "Hamlet" by William Shakespeare, act 1 scene 2 lines 127-128
# A [[carousal]]; a festival; a drinking frolic.
#* Tennyson
#*: Fill the cup, and fill the can, / Have a '''rouse''' before the morn.
# {{context|military|British|and|Canada|lang=en}} The [[sounding]] of a [[bugle]] in the morning after [[reveille]], to [[signal]] that soldiers are to [[rise]] from [[bed]], often ''the rouse''.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|rous|ing}}

# to [[wake]] or be [[awoken]] from [[sleep]], or from [[apathy]]
#: ''Night's black agents to their preys do '''rouse'''.'' &mdash; Shakespeare.
#: ''Morpheus '''rouses''' from his bed.'' &mdash; Alexander Pope.
#{{senseid|en|provoke}}to [[provoke]] (someone) to [[anger]] or [[action]]
# {{context|nautical|lang=en}} To [[pull]] by main [[strength]]; to [[haul]]
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} To be excited to thought or action from a state of [[indolence]] or [[inattention]].

